---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SendLab from "../../components/SendLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("send-receive");
const TR = "module/zfs/dmu_traverse.c";
const SEND = "module/zfs/dmu_send.c";
const RECV = "module/zfs/dmu_recv.c";
const IOC = "include/sys/zfs_ioctl.h";
const L = {
  record: gh(IOC, 246, 259),
  types: gh(IOC, 247, 252),
  begin: gh(IOC, 235, 244),
  magic: gh(IOC, 171),
  overlay: gh(IOC, 374, 387),
  size: gh(SEND, 266, 267),
  logical: gh(TR, 80, 81),
  getBirth: gh(TR, 78, 84),
  birthComment: gh("include/sys/spa.h", 483, 488),
  minTxg: gh(TR, 653),
  holesDefault: gh(TR, 44),
  fromtxg: gh(SEND, 2370),
  flags: gh(SEND, 2088),
  doDump: gh(SEND, 2568, 2574),
  dnodeLoop: gh(TR, 402, 404),
  objectRange: gh(SEND, 894, 898),
  dumpDnode: gh(SEND, 732, 742),
  bonus: gh(SEND, 793),
  freeEnd: gh(SEND, 796, 799),
  dumpFree: gh(SEND, 367, 403),
  freeAtOnce: gh(SEND, 396, 398),
  writeFlush: gh(SEND, 482, 486),
  freeobjMerge: gh(SEND, 683, 698),
  maxobj: gh(SEND, 653, 666),
  holeMeta: gh(SEND, 1012, 1017),
  holeFile: gh(SEND, 1018, 1034),
  fullFrees: gh(SEND, 326, 328),
  payloadSum: gh(SEND, 295, 298),
  end: gh(SEND, 2619, 2626),
  guids: gh(SEND, 2049, 2050),
  enodev: gh(RECV, 463, 479),
  recvBegin: gh(RECV, 1304, 1306),
  recvPayload: gh(RECV, 2813),
  queue: gh(RECV, 3500, 3513),
  endCheck: gh(RECV, 3000, 3007),
  cleanup: gh(RECV, 3582, 3588),
  writer: gh(RECV, 3285, 3294),
  recvObject: gh(RECV, 1968, 1989),
  recvFreeobjects: gh(RECV, 2208, 2215),
  recvFree: gh(RECV, 2666, 2684),
  recvFreeCase: gh(RECV, 3235, 3240),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="send-receive"
  title="send / receive · Uberblok"
  description="How zfs send finds the difference between two snapshots from birth txgs alone, skipping unchanged subtrees whole, and writes it as a stream that checks itself: interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          Keeping a copy of a filesystem on another machine looks simple: compare the two sides and copy what differs.
          But comparing means reading. To find the few blocks that changed since yesterday, a file-by-file tool reads or
          at least lists every file on both sides, every time, and it can only see a change through the contents it
          compares or the dates and sizes it trusts.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS does not compare. Every block pointer records the txg in which its block was born, and a snapshot taken in
          txg F holds no block born after F. So the difference between two snapshots is the blocks of the newer one born
          after the older one’s txg. Copy-on-write rewrites every block above a changed one, so a subtree with an old root
          has not changed below: one comparison at its top skips it whole, unread. What the traversal finds becomes a
          <em>stream</em> of records, each but the first carrying a checksum of everything before it, which the
          receiving side checks record by record, before applying each one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Send @s4, from the snapshot you choose</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <code>tank/fs</code> has four snapshots, one per txg of its history. The figure sends the last one,
          <code>@s4</code>: its tree, every block pointer with its logical birth txg. Choose the from-snapshot the
          receiver already holds, or none for a full stream, then play the send step by step: at each block pointer the
          birth is compared with F, the from-snapshot’s txg, records drop into the stream with their checksums, and the
          receiver checks and applies them until its files are those of <code>@s4</code>. “Flip a bit” damages the
          stream on the way.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SendLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          An incremental send walks the newer snapshot’s tree and skips every block pointer born at or before the older
          snapshot’s txg, with everything below it, and writes what is left as a stream of records whose running
          checksum the receiver verifies before replaying them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>zfs send -i @from @to</code> traverses the tree of <code>@to</code> with a lower bound, F, the txg in which
          <code>@from</code> was created. A block pointer born at or before F was already in <code>@from</code>, and so
          was the whole subtree under it, since <a href={roomUrl(lang, "copy-on-write")}>copy-on-write</a> gives a new
          birth to every block above a change: the traversal returns without reading it. Everything it does visit
          becomes records: an OBJECT for each dnode it meets, a WRITE for each data block, a FREE for each hole,
          FREEOBJECTS for each empty dnode slot. The stream starts with BEGIN, which names both snapshots by GUID, and
          ends with END. Every record but BEGIN carries the fletcher-4 checksum of every byte of the stream up to that checksum;
          the receiver keeps the same sum and refuses the stream at the first header that disagrees, before applying
          the record it follows. With no from-snapshot, F is 0, every block is visited, and the stream rebuilds the
          whole filesystem.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <strong>A stream of records.</strong> A send stream is a sequence of <a href={L.record}><code>dmu_replay_record_t</code></a>,
            each a 312-byte header with <a href={L.types}>one of eleven types</a>, sometimes followed by a payload: the
            data of a WRITE follows its header. BEGIN carries the magic number <a href={L.magic}><code>0x2F5bacbacULL</code></a>,
            <a href={L.begin}>the GUID of the snapshot sent and of the one it starts from</a>.
          </p>
          <p><strong>The bound.</strong> The traversal that feeds a send starts here.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SEND}
          from={1203}
          to={1221}
          symbol="send_traverse_thread"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 1211,
              to: 1213,
              text: `The dataset is traversed from <code>fromtxg</code>, the from-snapshot’s <a href="${L.fromtxg}">creation txg</a>, 0 for a full stream. <code>TRAVERSE_LOGICAL</code> joins the <a href="${L.flags}">flags of the send</a>, among them <code>TRAVERSE_PRE</code>: a block’s callback runs before its children.`,
            },
            {
              from: 1217,
              to: 1218,
              text: `An end-of-stream marker follows the last range. The ranges <code>send_cb</code> queues reach the main thread through more queues, in order, and <a href="${L.doDump}">become records there</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>The test that skips a subtree.</strong> Every block pointer of the snapshot goes through this.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={TR}
          from={249}
          to={279}
          symbol="traverse_visitbp"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 249,
              to: 276,
              text: `A hole of birth 0 was never written, or was made before the <code>hole_birth</code> feature. These lines would skip it in some cases, but only when <code>send_holes_without_birth_time</code> is 0, and it is <a href="${L.holesDefault}">1 by default</a>: such holes are visited. The figure has none; a hole punched in b carries the txg of the free, and goes through the test below.`,
            },
            {
              from: 277,
              to: 278,
              text: `The rule. <code>td_min_txg</code> is <a href="${L.minTxg}">the bound</a>, F. Born at or before it, the block is in the from-snapshot: the function returns before reading it or calling the callback, so nothing below it is reached. In the figure the chip turns dashed and its subtree fades.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <strong>Logical birth.</strong> <code>get_birth_time</code> <a href={L.getBirth}>returns the logical birth</a>
            when <code>TRAVERSE_LOGICAL</code> is set, as it is for a send. A block pointer has two births, and
            <a href={L.birthComment}>the comment that defines them</a> says the physical one can be smaller than the
            logical one for dedup and block cloning, and bigger for remapped and rewritten blocks, and that the logical one is
            the one for incremental replication. Send <a href={L.logical}>compares the logical one</a>, the txg in which
            the user changed the data: a block deduplicated or cloned onto an older copy is still sent, since the user
            wrote it after F, and a block that was only rewritten or remapped on disk is not resent.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Every dnode of a dnode block.</strong> When a dnode block is visited, each of its dnodes is <a href={L.dnodeLoop}>handed to this</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={TR}
          from={534}
          to={550}
          symbol="traverse_dnode"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 534,
              to: 543,
              text: "The callback runs for the dnode itself. No birth is tested here: every dnode of a visited dnode block reaches <code>send_cb</code>, changed or not.",
            },
            {
              from: 545,
              to: 550,
              text: "Then each of the dnode’s block pointers goes through <code>traverse_visitbp</code>, and its test.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            That is why, sending <code>@s4</code> from <code>@s3</code>, the stream holds OBJECT records for a and b,
            whose data did not change: deleting c rewrote the dnode block they share with it. Their data blocks are
            still skipped, one level down.
          </p>
          <p><strong>From visited blocks to records.</strong> The callback decides what each visit produces.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SEND}
          from={1098}
          to={1120}
          symbol="send_cb"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 1098,
              to: 1108,
              text: "A dnode becomes an OBJECT range, except the meta-dnode, object 0.",
            },
            {
              from: 1109,
              to: 1116,
              text: `A dnode block becomes an OBJECT_RANGE, which <a href="${L.objectRange}">only a raw stream writes</a>.`,
            },
            {
              from: 1117,
              to: 1120,
              text: "The objset block and every indirect block that is not a hole make no record: they are read only to reach what is below them.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SEND}
          from={1130}
          to={1146}
          symbol="send_cb"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 1130,
              to: 1135,
              text: "Nothing past the end of the object.",
            },
            {
              from: 1140,
              to: 1146,
              text: `What is left is a data block (a WRITE) or a hole (a FREE of <a href="${L.holeFile}">its range</a>; a hole among the dnode blocks <a href="${L.holeMeta}">frees objects</a>). REDACT belongs to redacted sends.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            An OBJECT range goes to <a href={L.dumpDnode}><code>dump_dnode</code></a>. A dnode slot with no object in it
            becomes FREEOBJECTS; a file becomes OBJECT, with its bonus buffer as <a href={L.bonus}>payload</a>, followed
            at once by <a href={L.freeEnd}>a FREE from the file’s end to <code>DMU_OBJECT_END</code></a>, so that a
            receiver whose copy was longer cuts it. <a href={L.dumpFree}><code>dump_free</code></a> keeps a FREE pending and
            merges the next one into it when it continues it, which is why the two holes of b travel as one FREE; a FREE
            to the end is <a href={L.freeAtOnce}>written at once</a>, and any other record <a href={L.writeFlush}>writes
            the pending one first</a>. FREEOBJECTS are <a href={L.freeobjMerge}>merged the same way</a>, and objects
            past the last dnode block <a href={L.maxobj}>are dropped</a>. A full stream carries frees too,
            <a href={L.fullFrees}>so that it can be received as a clone</a>.
          </p>
          <p><strong>The checksum that runs through the stream.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={SEND}
          from={257}
          to={283}
          symbol="dump_record"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 268,
              to: 270,
              text: "The running fletcher-4 sum is carried on over the record, up to its last 32 bytes.",
            },
            {
              from: 271,
              to: 277,
              text: `Except for BEGIN, that value goes into those 32 bytes, <code>drr_checksum</code>, <a href="${L.overlay}">overlaid on every record type</a>.`,
            },
            {
              from: 281,
              to: 283,
              text: `Then the 32 bytes are summed, and <a href="${L.payloadSum}">the payload</a> after them. So each record’s <code>drr_checksum</code> is the fletcher-4 of every byte of the stream before it, its own header up to the checksum included. The figure shows the low 32 bits of its last word.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            END carries one more: <a href={L.end}><code>drr_end.drr_checksum</code></a>, the checksum of everything
            before it.
          </p>
          <p><strong>The receiving side checks before it applies.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={RECV}
          from={2843}
          to={2868}
          symbol="receive_read_payload_and_next_header"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 2843,
              to: 2851,
              text: `The receiver keeps its own sum: it <a href="${L.recvBegin}">starts over the whole BEGIN record</a>, goes on <a href="${L.recvPayload}">over each payload</a>, and here over the next header, up to its checksum.`,
            },
            {
              from: 2861,
              to: 2866,
              text: "A header whose <code>drr_checksum</code> disagrees stops the receive: <code>ECKSUM</code>. A checksum of all zeros is not checked.",
            },
            {
              from: 2868,
              text: "The stored value is then summed, as the sender did.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            This runs when a record’s payload and the header after it have been read, and only then is the record
            <a href={L.queue}>queued for the writer</a>: a record whose bytes were damaged is never applied. END is
            checked once more, <a href={L.endCheck}>its <code>drr_end.drr_checksum</code> against the sum before it</a>.
            On an error, <a href={L.cleanup}>what the receive created is destroyed</a>: the receiver still holds the
            from-snapshot. Before any of this, an incremental stream’s BEGIN <a href={L.guids}>names the from-snapshot by
            GUID</a>, and the receiver <a href={L.enodev}>refuses it (<code>ENODEV</code>) unless it holds a snapshot with
            that GUID</a>.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Records dispatched by type.</strong> The writer thread <a href={L.writer}>takes each record off the queue</a> and hands it here.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={RECV}
          from={3188}
          to={3213}
          symbol="receive_process_record"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 3189,
              to: 3196,
              text: `OBJECT goes to <code>receive_object</code>, which <a href="${L.recvObject}">creates the object, or keeps one that exists</a>.`,
            },
            {
              from: 3197,
              to: 3203,
              text: `FREEOBJECTS goes to <code>receive_freeobjects</code>, which <a href="${L.recvFreeobjects}">frees each object of the range that exists</a>: c disappears.`,
            },
            {
              from: 3204,
              to: 3206,
              text: `WRITE goes to <code>receive_process_write_record</code>. FREE, <a href="${L.recvFreeCase}">further down</a>, goes to <code>receive_free</code>, which <a href="${L.recvFree}">frees the range in the object</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <strong>What the receiver ends with.</strong> Starting from the from-snapshot’s files, the records of the
            stream bring back exactly <code>@s4</code>: the figure compares the two at the end, for every from-snapshot,
            and its tests check it against the hand-worked records of each stream.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure is small: files of four 512-byte blocks under one indirect block, dnode blocks of four dnodes (a real
        16 KiB dnode block holds 32), a meta-dnode drawn with its two dnode blocks, no bonus buffer. The send runs as one
        sequence where the code joins several threads (traversal, merge, reader, the main thread) by queues that keep the
        order, and the receiver starts once the stream is complete, where the code receives through a pipe as the stream
        is written. Record headers are laid out as <code>dmu_replay_record_t</code>, with only the fields the figure
        uses filled in. Redacted, raw (encrypted), compressed and resumable streams, embedded data, WRITE_BYREF and spill
        blocks are not shown; neither are throughput figures, which depend on the disks and the network, not on this
        code.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="send-receive" />
</Base>

